--TEST--
`Core\Response::html` and `echo` are the two spellings of one HTML body, so they write to one output and put the same bytes there — `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink` and `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement rather than two plausible lines: `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape` makes `echo` the
// other way to write this same body, so the member and the sink handed the
// same carrier must answer the same bytes. A member that rendered the slot
// instead of writing it would still look right on one line alone.
var $name = "ada & <grace>";
Core\Html\Markup $fragment = html`<span>{$name}</span>`;
Core\Response::html($fragment);
echo "\n";
echo $fragment, "\n";

// One output and not two buffers, which is observable as ordering: what the
// member wrote and what `echo` wrote come back in the order they were written.
echo "before ";
Core\Response::html("<i>middle</i>" as Core\Html\Markup);
echo " after", "\n";

// One member called twice is a handler writing its whole body one way, which
// `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member` admits and a second body member beside it is what
// it refuses: the fragments accumulate in call order rather than the last one
// replacing the first.
Core\Response::html(html`<li>one</li>`);
Core\Response::html(html`<li>two</li>`);
echo "\n";

// The same claim under `Core\Out::capture`, which redirects that one output: a
// capture in force takes both writers or neither, and taking only `echo` would
// be the second buffer this design does not have.
var $captured = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    echo "a";
    Core\Response::html("<hr>" as Core\Html\Markup);
    echo "c";
});
echo $captured, "\n";
--EXPECT--
<span>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</span>
<span>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</span>
before <i>middle</i> after
<li>one</li><li>two</li>
a<hr>c
